    treated</em> in 1866. Now try making both components 10: the magnitude readout shows 14 —
    no Prussia possessed that army. Unification was the art of never pointing the arrow both
    ways at once.
  </p>

  <h2>The war with France, 1870–71</h2>
  <p>
    The southern states had stayed out of the Confederation, tied to Prussia by trade and
    held back by fear of France. Bismarck needed a war that would make them run to him — and
    Napoleon III provided it. In 1869–70 the Spanish throne was offered to Leopold, a prince
    of the Catholic Hohenzollern line; France, horrified at Hohenzollerns on two frontiers,
    protested until Wilhelm I withdrew the candidacy. But the French ambassador was then sent
    to demand, at Bad Ems, a guarantee that it would never be revived. Wilhelm refused
    politely and wired an account home; Bismarck abridged the telegram, published it on
    13 July 1870, and made it read as an insult to France — "a red rag to the Gallic bull".
    A jingoistic Parisian press pushed Napoleon III into declaring war on
    19 July. No ally came: Austria hesitated, Russia remembered Poland, Italy resented Rome,
    Britain stayed out.
  </p>
  <p>
    The French army was outmanned and out-organised; Prussia's mobilisation by rail was
    flawless, France's a shambles. After defeats at Wörth, Mars-la-Tour and Gravelotte, the
    Army of Châlons was trapped at Sedan: on 1–2 September 1870 Napoleon III surrendered with
    some 83,000 men. A republic was proclaimed in Paris on 4 September, but the war went on —
    Metz capitulated on 27 October with around 170,000 men, and Paris endured a four-month
    siege before surrendering in January 1871. On 18 January 1871,
    in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, the German princes proclaimed Wilhelm I German
    Emperor: a deliberate wound, inflicted in the palace of France's own kings. The Treaty of
    Frankfurt (10 May 1871) confirmed it — France ceded Alsace and most of Lorraine
    (including Metz and Strasbourg) and owed 5 billion francs, with German troops occupying
    the country until payment. The southern states had already joined: the war they feared
    had delivered them, gift-wrapped, to Prussia.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the telegram alone caused the war. Bismarck edited the Ems
    dispatch, but he did not invent the crisis — the candidacy was real, and French alarm at
    it was strategically rational. What the telegram did was convert a withdrawal into a
    public humiliation at the exact moment French opinion demanded glory. Wars need spark and
    fuel; Bismarck supplied only the spark.
  </p>

  <h2>How far was Bismarck responsible?</h2>  <p>
    Weigh Bismarck against the structures. The Zollverein, the railways, the army reforms
    (Roon and Moltke began before he took office), the organised nationalism of the
    Nationalverein, and the diplomatic conjuncture — Russia weakened by Crimea (1856) and
    distracted by Poland (1863), Austria humiliated in Italy (1859), France complicit in its
    own isolation — all pointed towards a Prussian-led Germany. Without Bismarck, unification
    might have happened anyway; without him it might equally have stalled for a generation.
    What he decided were the things at the margin: the timing, the <em>kleindeutsch</em> form
    (Austria excluded for good), the leniency to Austria, the baiting of France, and —
    catastrophically — the annexation of Alsace-Lorraine against the advice of Moltke and the
    Crown Prince. The fairest verdict: unification was overdetermined, but Bismarck chose its
    speed, shape and price.
  </p>

  <h2>What unification meant for Europe</h2>
  <p>
    Italian unification mattered most to Austria (expelled from Italy, though it kept the
    Italian-speaking Trento and Trieste that nationalists called <em>Italia irredenta</em>)
    and to France, which gained Nice and Savoy but spent a decade defending the Pope — a
    growing source of Franco-Italian friction until 1870 removed the garrison. Britain was
    broadly sympathetic. German unification
    was the earthquake. Austria, expelled from Germany, reorganised as the Dual Monarchy with
    Hungary (1867) and turned south-east. Denmark and France had lost territory; France gained
    a permanent grievance — <em>revanche</em> — that drove its diplomacy for forty years.
    Above all, a new empire of some 41 million people now fielded the best army in Europe.
    Bismarck spent the next two decades building alliances precisely because unification had
    frightened everyone; Alsace-Lorraine remained the fault line along which Europe cracked
    in 1914.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Notice on the timeline that the two unifications were not parallel events but
    <em>coupled</em> ones: 1859, 1864, 1866 and 1870–71 form a single chain in which each
    settlement set up the next. Villafranca's unfinished business (Venetia) drew Italy into
    1866 on Prussia's side; the leniency of 1866 kept Austria neutral in 1870; Rome fell in
    September 1870 only because France's garrison was needed at Sedan. Even Italy's completion
    was a by-product of Germany's method — one reason historians treat 1848–71 as a single
    process, nationalism reshaping Central Europe through the great-power system.
  </p>
  <p>
    The new states carried costs that triumphal maps hide. The Kingdom of Italy began with a
    franchise of under 2 per cent (about 421,000 voters out of 22 million), a decade-long
    campaign against "brigandage" in the south, and a north-south wealth gap (the
    <em>questione meridionale</em>). The German Empire was a constitutional oddity: a Kaiser
    controlling the army, a Bundesrat where Prussia held 17 of 58 votes, and a Reichstag
    elected by universal male suffrage that could not dismiss governments. Historians call
    1871 a "revolution from above": the nation was made, but parliament was not sovereign —
    an imbalance that shaped German politics until 1918.
  </p>

